# Reading real REBOL source into values.
#
# Every entry here is a line lifted from the fourteen programs in
# corpus/sources/, cited by file and line. Nothing is invented. The point
# of loader tests drawn from real code rather than from a grammar is that
# real code uses the awkward combinations, and a hand-written example
# tends not to.
#
# The `types` field lists the datatype of each value the text loads to, in
# order. A count mismatch fails the entry just as a type mismatch does,
# which is what catches a reader that silently merges or splits values.

# --- Pairs and tuples ---------------------------------------
# 184 pairs and 90 tuples across all fourteen files. View code is built
# from coordinates and colours, so these two carry most of the traffic.

--- id loading/pair-literal
--- origin corpus/sources/calculator.r line 29
--- code
40x24
--- types
pair

--- id loading/pair-in-context
--- origin corpus/sources/color-names.r line 19
--- note A pair sits between an integer and a block with no delimiters
--- note around it, so the reader must end the integer at the x.
--- code
font-size 11 100x38 [sc/color: face/color show sc]
--- types
word integer pair block

--- id loading/tuple-three-segments
--- origin corpus/sources/tile-game.r line 10
--- code
100.150.150
--- types
tuple

--- id loading/tuple-with-zero-segments
--- origin corpus/sources/clock1.r line 9
--- note Leading zeros in every segment, which is where a reader that
--- note parses tuples as decimals first goes wrong.
--- code
gradient 0x1 0.0.150 0.0.50
--- types
word pair tuple tuple

--- id loading/version-tuple
--- origin corpus/sources/color-names.r line 3
--- code
1.0.1
--- types
tuple

--- id loading/pair-arithmetic-is-two-values
--- origin corpus/sources/tile-game.r line 9
--- note The multiplication is evaluation, not lexis. Reading gives three
--- note values and it is the evaluator that turns them into a pair.
--- code
3 * 40x40
--- types
integer word pair

# --- Decimals -----------------------------------------------

--- id loading/decimal-with-leading-dot
--- origin corpus/sources/font-lab.r line 84
--- note .5 with no leading zero. One dot makes a decimal; the tuple
--- note entries above are the same reader decision going the other way.
--- code
sl1/data: .5
--- types
set-path decimal

--- id loading/decimal-among-operators
--- origin corpus/sources/color-names.r line 46
--- code
m: max 1 to-integer factor - .5 * 8
--- types
set-word word integer word word word decimal word integer

# --- Paths --------------------------------------------------
# 478 paths across all fourteen files, and they are where the reader has
# to be most careful.

--- id loading/simple-path
--- origin corpus/sources/calculator.r line 15
--- code
text-box/color: red
--- types
set-path word

--- id loading/set-path-with-get-word-segment
--- origin corpus/sources/color-names.r line 51
--- note The hardest single line in these fourteen files. A set-path whose
--- note third segment is a get-word: window, pane, :n, color, and the
--- note trailing colon belongs to the whole path rather than to color.
--- code
window/pane/:n/color: clr
--- types
set-path word

--- id loading/path-with-get-word-segment
--- origin corpus/sources/mines.r line 79
--- code
grid/pane/:index/gui-state: 'cleared
--- types
set-path lit-word

--- id loading/path-read-not-assigned
--- origin corpus/sources/mines.r line 95
--- code
state: grid/pane/:index/gui-state
--- types
set-word path

--- id loading/path-with-integer-segment
--- origin corpus/sources/clock.r line 16
--- note A path segment that is a word, and a set-path ending in a word
--- note that could be read as a refinement if the reader is careless.
--- code
b/font/size: max 24 f/size/y - 40
--- types
set-path word integer path word integer

--- id loading/path-then-comment
--- origin corpus/sources/clock.r line 18
--- note The comment produces no value. A reader that returns one for it
--- note would shift everything after it.
--- code
b/size/x: 1024 ; for size-text
--- types
set-path integer

# --- Words in their various shapes --------------------------

--- id loading/refinement-in-a-function-spec
--- origin corpus/sources/color-names.r line 43
--- note A refinement is a slash with nothing before it. A path is a slash
--- note with something before it. Both appear on this line.
--- code
[factor /local clr n m d]
--- types
block

--- id loading/lit-word
--- origin corpus/sources/color-names.r line 26
--- code
append out 'return
--- types
word word lit-word

--- id loading/modulo-is-an-ordinary-word
--- origin corpus/sources/color-names.r line 26
--- note // is a word like any other, not punctuation. This is why the
--- note reader can be ignorant of operators entirely.
--- code
zero? cnt // 6
--- types
word word word integer

--- id loading/word-with-question-mark
--- origin corpus/sources/mines.r line 81
--- code
not grid/pane/:index/data
--- types
word path

--- id loading/set-word-chain
--- origin corpus/sources/tile-game.r line 32
--- code
t15: tile "15"  t14: tile "14"
--- types
set-word word string set-word word string

# --- The scalar types that carry their own syntax -----------

--- id loading/file-literal
--- origin corpus/sources/tile-game.r line 6
--- code
%tile-game.r
--- types
file

--- id loading/url-and-file-on-one-line
--- origin corpus/sources/effect-lab.r line 10
--- note A url ends at whitespace, so the file after it is a separate
--- note value rather than part of the url.
--- code
read-thru/to http://www.rebol.com/view/demos/nyc.jpg %nyc.jpg
--- types
path url file

--- id loading/bare-email-literal
--- origin corpus/sources/mines.r line 4
--- code
Email: allenk@powerup.com.au
--- types
set-word email

--- id loading/quoted-email-is-a-string
--- origin corpus/sources/rebodex.r line 14
--- note The same characters in quotes are a string, not an email. The
--- note contrast with the previous entry is the whole test.
--- code
email "carl@rebol.com"
--- types
word string

--- id loading/date-literal
--- origin corpus/sources/tile-game.r line 3
--- code
Date: 15-May-2000
