# --- Whole structures ---------------------------------------

--- id loading/script-header
--- origin corpus/sources/clock1.r lines 1 to 5
--- note The header is a word followed by an ordinary block. It is not
--- note special syntax, which is why TRANSCODE can hand it back as
--- note values like anything else.
--- code
REBOL [
	Title: "Basic Digital Clock"
	Author: "Carl Sassenrath"
	Purpose: "A simple digital clock."
]
--- types
word block

--- id loading/nested-blocks-with-mixed-literals
--- origin corpus/sources/clock1.r lines 7 to 11
--- note The whole of the smallest demo, which between them covers pairs,
--- note tuples, integers, blocks, set-words and paths.
--- code
view layout [
	origin 0
	banner 140x32 rate 1 effect [gradient 0x1 0.0.150 0.0.50]
		feel [engage: func [f a e] [set-face f now/time]]
]
--- types
word word block

# --- Construction syntax ------------------------------------
#
# The values with no literal spelling of their own. This is what MOLD
# produces for them, so it is the syntax that makes them round-trip.
#
# REBOL 2 and R3-Alpha wrote these with square brackets, as #[none].
# Rebol 3.x replaced the form rather than adding to it and now refuses the
# bracket spelling. JEBOL read only the bracket form until Rebol's own
# test suite was run against it, which is when seventeen of twenty-two
# files turned out not to parse at all.
#
# Written as LOAD of a string rather than as bare source, so that a
# reader failure is something the entry can catch and name instead of
# stopping the whole script before it starts.

--- id loading/construct-none
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
type? load {#(none)}
--- result
#(none!)

--- id loading/construct-true
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
type? load {#(true)}
--- result
#(logic!)

--- id loading/construct-false-is-not-the-same-value-as-true
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
false = load {#(false)}
--- result
#(true)

--- id loading/construct-unset
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note The one that cannot be written any other way at all, and the one
--- note that stopped series-test.r3 on its twenty-sixth line.
--- code
type? load {#(unset)}
--- result
#(unset!)

--- id loading/construct-naming-a-datatype
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note A datatype value, not an integer. #(integer!) is the datatype
--- note itself, which is what makes datatypes usable as parse rules and
--- note as type declarations in a function spec.
--- code
type? load {#(integer!)}
--- result
#(datatype!)

--- id loading/construct-naming-a-datatype-that-does-not-exist
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note One failure covers both ways a construct can be wrong: a datatype
--- note that does not exist and a datatype that cannot be built from what
--- note follows both give malconstruct.
--- code
load {#(nonesuch!)}
--- error syntax malconstruct

--- id loading/construct-building-a-value-from-contents
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note The third shape: build a value of the named datatype from what
--- note follows. This reads as the decimal 1.0 and not as the integer 1,
--- note which is the whole point of writing it.
--- code
type? load {#(decimal! 1)}
--- result
#(decimal!)

--- id loading/a-datatype-that-cannot-be-built-that-way
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note Not every datatype accepts contents, so the reader has to know
--- note which do rather than assuming any of them will.
--- code
load {#(char! 65)}
--- error syntax malconstruct

--- id loading/the-square-bracket-form-no-longer-means-a-construct
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader map
--- note R3-Alpha's spelling for a construct. The brackets were not freed
--- note up but reused: #[1 2] is a map! in Rebol 3.x, and #[none] is a
--- note malformed map rather than a malformed construct.
--- note
--- note A malformed map answers invalid-arg, not malconstruct. R3 reads
--- note the brackets as key-value pairs and refuses an odd count as a bad
--- note argument, and lexer-test.r3 asserts that id for `#[x]`. The reader
--- note gives that id directly, so a script catches the odd map by id.
--- code
load {#[none]}
--- error syntax invalid-arg

# --- LOAD ---------------------------------------------------
#
# Reading source text at run time. Specced from the start and only
# implemented once Rebol's own suite needed it.

--- id loading/load-a-single-value-gives-that-value
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note Not a block wrapping it. This is the boundary that makes LOAD
--- note awkward to implement and pleasant to use.
--- code
type? load "1"
--- result
#(integer!)

--- id loading/load-two-values-gives-a-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
type? load "1 2"
--- result
#(block!)

--- id loading/load-nothing-gives-an-empty-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note The degenerate case goes the other way: no values is a block, not
--- note an unset, because there is no single value to unwrap to.
--- code
load ""
--- result
[]

--- id loading/load-a-block-gives-that-block-not-a-wrapper
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note One value that happens to be a block is still one value.
--- code
load "[1 2]"
--- result
[1 2]

--- id loading/load-all-always-gives-a-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note The refinement that turns the unwrapping off, for a caller who
--- note would rather not test what came back.
--- code
type? load/all "1"
--- result
#(block!)

# --- Datatype names the reader must know --------------------
#
# The reader knows every datatype spelling, whether or not values of that
# type can be built yet. A name it does not know stops the whole file, and
# Rebol's own suite showed what that costs: one `make bitset!` on line 394
# of series-test.r3 hid the other 1,138 assertions behind it.
#
# Reading the name is not a claim that the datatype works. A script that
# tries to make one of these still fails, which is the honest place for it
# to fail.

--- id loading/a-datatype-name-in-source-is-a-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note Not a datatype value. `bitset!` in source is an ordinary word that
--- note happens to be bound to a datatype, which is why `make bitset! ...`
--- note works without the reader knowing anything about bitsets. Only
--- note #(bitset!) is the datatype value itself.
--- code
type? load {bitset!}
--- result
#(word!)

--- id loading/an-unknown-datatype-name-is-also-just-a-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note The reader does not decide which datatypes exist. Anything ending
--- note in ! reads as a word and evaluation decides whether it names
--- note anything, which is why a reader that refuses unknown spellings is
--- note refusing valid source.
--- code
type? load {end!}
--- result
#(word!)

--- id loading/a-spelling-that-names-no-datatype-is-a-word-too
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
type? load {nonesuch!}
--- result
#(word!)

# --- ref! ---------------------------------------------------

--- id loading/an-at-sign-starts-a-ref
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note A datatype Rebol 3.x added. JEBOL read @bob as a word, which is
--- note the quiet kind of reader bug: it parses into the wrong thing
--- note rather than failing.
--- code
type? load {@bob}
--- result
#(ref!)

--- id loading/a-ref-molds-back-as-it-was-written
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
mold load {@bob}
--- result
"@bob"

--- id loading/an-at-sign-on-its-own-is-an-empty-ref
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note The degenerate case, and it is a ref rather than an error.
--- code
type? load {@}
--- result
#(ref!)

--- id loading/a-known-datatype-name-is-a-word-too
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note Not only the unknown ones. integer! is a word that the system
--- note context binds to a datatype, which is why a function spec and a
--- note parse rule both have to look the word up rather than expect the
--- note reader to have handed them a datatype.
--- code
type? load {integer!}
--- result
#(word!)

--- id loading/a-typeset-name-is-a-word-as-well
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
type? load {number!}
--- result
#(word!)

--- id loading/the-datatype-value-itself-still-needs-a-construct
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note The contrast that makes the rule above mean something.
--- code
type? load {#(integer!)}
--- result
#(datatype!)

--- id loading/a-datatype-word-evaluates-to-its-datatype
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note Which is what makes reading it as a word harmless: the value you
--- note get by evaluating it is the one the reader used to produce.
--- code
datatype? integer!
--- result
#(true)

# --- Tags, and the words that look like them ----------------

--- id loading/an-angle-run-holding-a-name-is-a-tag
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
type? load {<a>}
--- result
#(tag!)

--- id loading/an-angle-run-of-only-symbols-is-a-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note Closing with > is not the test. JEBOL used it and made <--> a
--- note tag, which cost lexer-test.r3 all 444 of its assertions on the
--- note line that asserts this exact case.
--- code
type? load {<-->}
--- result
#(word!)

--- id loading/the-not-equal-operator-is-a-word-not-an-empty-tag
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
type? load {<>}
--- result
#(word!)

--- id loading/a-comparison-operator-is-a-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
type? load {<=}
--- result
#(word!)

--- id loading/a-tag-may-hold-digits
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note One non-symbol character is enough to make it a tag.
--- code
type? load {<3>}
--- result
#(tag!)

--- id loading/a-closing-tag-is-a-tag
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
type? load {</a>}
--- result
#(tag!)

# --- Caret escapes ------------------------------------------

--- id loading/a-caret-space-is-a-space
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note The escape JEBOL refused. Three characters, not four.
--- code
length? "a^ b"
--- result
3

# --- Objects in construction syntax -------------------------

--- id loading/a-construct-can-build-an-object
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader object
--- note The single most valuable construct to read: series-test.r3 and
--- note object-test.r3 hold 1,725 assertions between them and both stop
--- note at the first one of these. The block is taken as written and
--- note never evaluated, because the reader has no context to evaluate in.
--- code
type? load {#(object! [a: 1])}
--- result
#(object!)

--- id loading/an-object-built-by-a-construct-holds-its-fields
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader object
--- code
built: load {#(object! [a: 1])}
built/a
--- result
1

--- id loading/two-objects-built-the-same-way-are-equal
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader object
--- code
equal? load {#(object! [a: 1])} load {#(object! [a: 1])}
--- result
#(true)

# --- Integers in another base -------------------------------

--- id loading/a-base-two-integer
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note Digits before the hash are the base and what follows is the
--- note number in it. Without this the whole run is an invalid lexeme,
--- note which cost lexer-test.r3 thirty-five assertions.
--- code
transcode/one "2#01"
--- result
1

--- id loading/a-base-sixteen-integer
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
transcode/one "16#FF"
--- result
255

--- id loading/a-base-ten-integer-is-still-an-integer
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
transcode/one "10#42"
--- result
42

--- id loading/a-bare-percent-is-a-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note With nothing after it there is no filename, so it is the word the
--- note modulo operator is bound to. Reading it as an empty file made
--- note `7 % 0` answer 0 rather than dividing by zero.
--- code
type? first [%]
--- result
#(word!)

--- id loading/a-percent-with-a-name-after-it-is-a-file
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
type? first [%a]
--- result
#(file!)

--- id loading/a-construct-names-a-datatype-that-has-no-values-yet
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note The name has to exist even though nothing can be made of it.
--- note A bare bitset! in source is a word and the reader decides
--- note nothing about it; #(bitset!) asks for the datatype itself, and
--- note that is a different question.
--- code
type? load {#(bitset!)}
--- result
#(datatype!)

--- id loading/a-construct-can-build-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
load {#(string! "ab")}
--- result
"ab"

--- id loading/a-construct-can-build-a-file
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note And the datatype it names is the one it gets, so this is a file
--- note rather than the string it was built from.
--- code
load {#(file! "ab")}
--- result
%ab

--- id loading/a-base-two-integer-fills-the-whole-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note Sixty-four ones is minus one: the digits fill the machine word and
--- note the top bit is the sign, so this is read unsigned and then taken
--- note as signed.
--- code
transcode/one "2#1111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111"
--- result
-1

--- id loading/a-base-eight-integer-fills-the-whole-word-too
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
transcode/one "8#7777777777777777777777"
--- result
-1

--- id loading/a-digit-the-base-does-not-have
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note Two is not a base-two digit, and the whole lexeme is refused
--- note rather than the number stopping short.
--- code
load "2#12"
--- error syntax invalid

--- id loading/a-letter-where-base-ten-expects-a-digit
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
load "10#1A2"
--- error syntax invalid

--- id loading/a-based-integer-too-big-for-the-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note Past sixty-four bits there is nowhere to put it, so it is a
--- note syntax error rather than a wrapped number.
--- code
load "10#9999999999999999999"
--- error syntax invalid

--- id loading/base-zero-means-hexadecimal
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note Zero is not a base, so it stands for the default one. Refusing it
--- note cost mold-test.r3 eighteen assertions, and the regression guard
--- note caught that rather than the count quietly dropping.
--- code
0#FF
--- result
255

--- id loading/base-zero-fills-the-word-like-any-bit-pattern
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
0#8000000000000000
--- result
-9223372036854775808

# --- The control-character escapes --------------------------
#
# A caret before a character from @ to _ escapes to that character less
# sixty-four, which is how the control codes are written. The caret
# itself is the exception: ^^ is a caret and not thirty.
#
# JEBOL handled only the letters, so ^[ was an invalid escape. That one
# omission is what stopped most of Rebol's own library from loading.

--- id loading/caret-open-bracket-is-escape
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note ESC, and the escape Rebol's own library uses most.
--- code
to integer! first "^["
--- result
27

--- id loading/caret-at-sign-is-nul
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note The bottom of the range.
--- code
to integer! first "^@"
--- result
0

--- id loading/caret-underscore-is-the-top-of-the-range
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
to integer! first "^_"
--- result
31

--- id loading/caret-caret-is-a-caret-not-thirty
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note The exception in the middle of the range: a caret has to be able
--- note to mean itself.
--- code
to integer! first "^^"
--- result
94

--- id loading/caret-tilde-is-delete
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note Outside the range, and its own case.
--- code
to integer! first "^~"
--- result
127

--- id loading/an-escape-outside-the-control-range-is-the-character-itself
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note There is no such thing as an unknown escape for a character that
--- note is there: a caret before anything the table does not name gives
--- note that character back. JEBOL refused these, which is what stopped
--- note codec-css.reb and others in Rebol's own library.
--- code
to integer! first "^="
--- result
61

--- id loading/an-escaped-digit-is-that-digit
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- code
to integer! first "^1"
--- result
49

--- id loading/an-escaped-space-is-a-space
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires reader
--- note Which the earlier entry for a caret-space said, and this is the
--- note rule it turns out to be a case of rather than an exception.
--- code
to integer! first "^ "
--- result
32

--- id loading/molding-the-null-character
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Control characters mold as a caret and the letter 64 above them,
--- note so code point 0 is ^@ and 1 is ^A. This is the bottom of that
--- note range.
--- code
mold to char! 0
--- result
{#"^^@"}

--- id loading/molding-the-last-control-character
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note 31 is the top of the caret-letter range; 32 is a space and
--- note molds as itself.
--- code
mold to char! 31
--- result
{#"^^_"}

--- id loading/molding-the-first-printable-character
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold to char! 32
--- result
{#" "}

--- id loading/molding-the-caret-itself
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The escape character has to escape itself.
--- code
mold to char! 94
--- result
{#"^^^^"}

--- id loading/molding-past-the-printable-range
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note 127 is where the caret-letter form stops and the hex form
--- note starts, and the hex is upper case.
--- code
mold to char! 127
--- result
{#"^^(7F)"}

--- id loading/converting-a-number-to-a-character
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
to char! 65
--- result
#"A"

--- id loading/converting-a-string-takes-its-first-character
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Rather than refusing a string of more than one, which is what
--- note the name suggests it might do.
--- code
to char! "AB"
--- result
#"A"

--- id loading/converting-a-decimal-truncates
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
to char! 65.9
--- result
#"A"

--- id loading/the-lowest-character
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
to char! 0
--- result
#"^@"

--- id loading/one-below-the-lowest-character-is-refused
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
to char! -1
--- error access invalid-char

--- id loading/one-past-the-highest-character-is-refused
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The top of Unicode, so the boundary is where the code points
--- note run out rather than anywhere REBOL chose. The failure is an
--- note ACCESS error rather than a script one, which is not where you
--- note would look for it.
--- code
to char! 1114112
--- error access invalid-char

--- id loading/converting-nothing-to-a-character-is-refused
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
to char! none
--- error script bad-make-arg

--- id loading/molding-the-code-point-that-would-clash-with-the-caret
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note 30 is the one hole in the caret-letter range. 30 plus 64 is the
--- note caret itself, so ^^ would read back as a caret rather than as
--- note 30, and R3 writes the hex form instead. Every other code point
--- note below 32 except tab and newline takes the letter.
--- code
mold to char! 30
--- result
{#"^^(1E)"}

--- id loading/molding-the-code-point-either-side-of-that-hole
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold to char! 29
--- result
{#"^^]"}

--- id loading/a-scheme-with-nothing-after-it-is-a-url
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note A colon makes a set-word only when nothing follows it. Here
--- note the slashes follow, so the whole lexeme is a url.
--- code
type? first [tls://]
--- result
#(url!)

--- id loading/a-url-may-end-in-a-colon
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note And a trailing colon does not turn it back into a set-word.
--- note Rebol's own TLS scheme is written `tls://:` and this one
--- note character stopped fifty-three of its definitions loading.
--- code
mold first [tls://:]
--- result
"tls://:"

--- id loading/a-colon-with-nothing-after-it-is-a-set-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The boundary the other way.
--- code
type? first [a:]
--- result
#(set-word!)

--- id loading/a-scheme-with-no-slashes-is-still-a-url
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
type? first [mailto:x]
--- result
#(url!)
